<h1>Dependency Injection (DI) in ASP.NET Core</h1>

<p>
    Dependency Injection is a design pattern where a class does not create its dependencies itself.
    Instead, dependencies are provided (injected) from outside.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Without DI (Bad Practice)</h2>

<pre>
<code>
public class StudentController
{
    private StudentService _service = new StudentService();
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Problems:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Tight coupling</li>
    <li>Hard to test</li>
    <li>Difficult to maintain</li>
    <li>Violates SOLID principles</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>With DI (Good Practice)</h2>

<pre>
<code>
public class StudentController
{
    private readonly IStudentService _service;

    public StudentController(IStudentService service)
    {
        _service = service;
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Benefits:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Loose coupling</li>
    <li>Easy testing (mocking possible)</li>
    <li>Better maintainability</li>
    <li>Follows SOLID principles</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Core Idea of DI</h2>

<pre>
<code>
Without DI → Class creates object ❌
With DI → Framework injects object ✔
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>DI Container</h2>

<p>
    ASP.NET Core has a built-in Dependency Injection container that:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Creates objects</li>
    <li>Manages object lifetime</li>
    <li>Injects dependencies automatically</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Service Registration (Program.cs)</h2>

<pre>
<code>
builder.Services.AddScoped<IStudentService, StudentService>();
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Types of Service Lifetimes</h2>

<h3>1. Transient</h3>

<pre>
<code>
builder.Services.AddTransient<IStudentService, StudentService>();
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    A new object is created every time it is requested.
</p>

<p>
    Use case: Lightweight stateless services
</p>

<hr />

<h3>2. Scoped (Most Used)</h3>

<pre>
<code>
builder.Services.AddScoped<IStudentService, StudentService>();
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    One object is created per HTTP request.
</p>

<p>
    Use case: Business services, Repository pattern
</p>

<hr />

<h3>3. Singleton</h3>

<pre>
<code>
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IStudentService, StudentService>();
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    One object is created for the entire application lifetime.
</p>

<p>
    Use case: Logging, caching, configuration services
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Real Example (Repository Pattern Style)</h2>

<h3>Step 1: Interface</h3>

<pre>
<code>
public interface IStudentRepository
{
    List<string> GetStudents();
}
</code>
</pre>

<h3>Step 2: Implementation</h3>

<pre>
<code>
public class StudentRepository : IStudentRepository
{
    public List<string> GetStudents()
    {
        return new List<string> { "A", "B", "C" };
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<h3>Step 3: Register Service</h3>

<pre>
<code>
builder.Services.AddScoped<IStudentRepository, StudentRepository>();
</code>
</pre>

<h3>Step 4: Inject in Controller</h3>

<pre>
<code>
public class StudentController : Controller
{
    private readonly IStudentRepository _repo;

    public StudentController(IStudentRepository repo)
    {
        _repo = repo;
    }

    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        var data = _repo.GetStudents();
        return View(data);
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Internal Working of DI</h2>

<pre>
<code>
Request comes
    ↓
DI container checks required services
    ↓
Creates objects
    ↓
Injects into controller
    ↓
Controller executes
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Where DI is Used in ASP.NET Core</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Controllers</li>
    <li>Middleware</li>
    <li>Filters</li>
    <li>Repositories</li>
    <li>Services</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>DI in Middleware Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
public class MyMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    private readonly ILogger<MyMiddleware> _logger;

    public MyMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, ILogger<MyMiddleware> logger)
    {
        _next = next;
        _logger = logger;
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Interview Points</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Dependency Injection is used to achieve loose coupling</li>
    <li>It removes responsibility of object creation from the class</li>
    <li>ASP.NET Core has built-in DI container</li>
    <li>3 lifetimes: Transient, Scoped, Singleton</li>
</ul>